        "knowledge_point": "Finding the First Terms of the Taylor Series of a Function About a Point",
        "problem": "Find the first four non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $e^{-x}$ centered about the point $x=3.$",
        "worked_solution": "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)\t= f(a) + f'(a)(x-a) + \\frac{f''(a)}{2!}(x-a)^2 +\\frac{f'''(a)}{3!}(x-a)^3+\\cdots,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=e^{-x}$ and $a=3.$\n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ f(3) & ={e}^{-3} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(3) & =-{e}^{-3} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(3) & ={e}^{-3} \\\\ {f}^{\u2034}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{\u2034}(3) & =-{e}^{-3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x} & ={e}^{-3}+(-{e}^{-3})(x-3)+\\frac{({e}^{-3})}{2!}(x-3)^{2}+\\frac{(-{e}^{-3})}{3!}(x-3)^{3}+\u22ef \\\\  & ={e}^{-3}-{e}^{-3}(x-3)+\\frac{{e}^{-3}}{2}(x-3)^{2}-\\frac{{e}^{-3}}{6}(x-3)^{3}+\u22ef\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
